+FloatLean and MPFR must agree at every binary width. Native C and SoftFloat join at binary32 and
+binary64, and CPython joins the binary64 operations it provides. The runner rejects the old
+counter-only trace and any missing or disagreeing agreement prefix.
+
+These numbers include fixture lookup and checksum work. They measure end-to-end scalar calls with
+one result-dependent chain, not reciprocal throughput and not the latency of a bare CPU
+instruction.
+
+The runner calibrates each lane, width, and operation toward a 200 ms measured interval. It
+shuffles one deterministic base order and uses a cyclic rotation for each trial, so no lane is
+always first or last. The complete schedule is retained as `environment/trial-order.csv`.
+
+## Formats and comparison points
+
+Posits use total storage widths 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048, and
+4096.
+
+Configured binary formats begin at four bits:
+
+- widths 4 through 8 use E2M1, E2M2, E3M2, E3M3, and E4M3;
+- widths 16, 32, 64, and 128 use the IEEE interchange layouts;
+- widths 256 and above use the documented E19 layout;
+- optional widths 24 and 48 use E8M15 and E11M36 and have no equal-width posit or external row.
+
+MPFR and Flocq use the significand precision of the binary format at the same storage width. This
+is a binary companion point. Posit precision changes with magnitude, so a posit/MPFR ratio is not
+an equal-precision claim. `ratios.csv` records the selected MPFR precision and the pairing rule.
+
+The 8-bit binary row is E4M3. It is not labelled IEEE binary8 because IEEE 754 does not define a
+binary8 interchange format.
+
