+base, and matching a benchmark workload is not a conformance proof. The independent validation
+campaign is documented in [`tests/EXTERNAL.md`](../../tests/EXTERNAL.md).
+
+## Publication protocol
+
+A public run uses:
+
+- deterministic exact-rational inputs rounded into the destination format before timing;
+- nonnegative square-root fixtures;
+- per-cell warmup and calibration;
+- at least seven trials, with nine used by the retained release campaign;
+- a minimum timed duration for every accepted public row;
+- one common result-dependent fixture chain across adapters;
+- one fixed 256-operation agreement prefix for every comparable binary adapter;
+- a pinned CPU when `FORMAT_COMPARE_CPU` is set;
+- recorded source revisions, compiler and library versions, CPU information, and environment;
+- exact adapter agreement checks before timing where a shared binary workload exists; and
+- explicit rejection records instead of silently substituting another implementation.
+
+Pinning the process to one scheduler-assigned CPU reduces migration noise. It does not prove that
+the process owned an exclusive physical core or that neighboring jobs could not affect shared
+caches, memory bandwidth, frequency, or thermals. The environment record says what was reserved
+and what affinity was applied.
+
+The FloatLean line is intentionally segmented by backend. Small tables, native-word kernels,
+configured proved word kernels, fixed-limb kernels, and exact-dyadic execution have different cost
+structures. The plots annotate those boundaries, while `backend-regimes.csv` gives the exact
+mapping. We keep one public API series because that is what a caller invokes, but we do not hide
+the implementation changes underneath it.
+
+## Running a fresh campaign
+
+Choose a new output directory. Do not write directly into the retained result tree:
+
+```bash
+FORMAT_COMPARE_DEVELOPMENT_ONLY=0 \
+FORMAT_COMPARE_INCLUDE_FLOCQ=1 \
+FORMAT_COMPARE_INCLUDE_UNIVERSAL=1 \
+FORMAT_COMPARE_INCLUDE_SOFTFLOAT=1 \
+FORMAT_COMPARE_INCLUDE_PYTHON=1 \
+FORMAT_COMPARE_BUILD_JOBS=16 \
+FORMAT_COMPARE_CPU=3 \
+benchmarks/scripts/format-comparison.sh \
+  9 \
+  /path/to/new-format-comparison
+```
+
+`FORMAT_COMPARE_BUILD_JOBS` controls only the `make -j` used to compile Berkeley SoftFloat; it does
+not set Lean, Lake, or campaign-wide parallelism. Set it within the machine's allocation when the
+SoftFloat lane is enabled. Set `FORMAT_COMPARE_CPU` to one CPU inside the job's assigned set; omit
+it only when the scheduler or runtime already supplies the required affinity. Flocq needs Docker,
+or an executable supplied with `FLOCQ_BENCH_BINARY`.
+
+The runner rejects publication settings with fewer than seven trials, disabled calibration, a
+nonstandard agreement-prefix length, counter-only fixture traces, stale CSV schemas, short timed
+rows, dirty external checkouts, a missing CPU pin, a disabled external lane, or an existing output
+path. For a deliberately short development run:
+
+```bash
+FORMAT_COMPARE_DEVELOPMENT_ONLY=1 \
+benchmarks/scripts/format-comparison.sh \
+  1 \
+  /path/to/new-diagnostic-run
+```
+
+Do not publish a development run as the release result.
+
+## Result layout
+
+The retained benchmark tree contains:
+
+| Path | Contents |
+| --- | --- |
+| `raw/` | One measured CSV row per implementation, format, operation, and trial |
+| `calibration/` | Warmup observations and selected iteration counts |
+| `environment/` | Host, toolchain, source revision, affinity, and binary-agreement records |
+| `resources/` | Per-process resource observations retained by the runner |
+| `plots/summary.csv` | Aggregated medians, quartiles, and median absolute deviations |
+| `plots/ratios.csv` | Explicitly paired performance ratios |
+| `plots/backend-regimes.csv` | Backend boundaries used to annotate the public plot |
+| `plots/format-comparison.*` | PNG, SVG, and PDF views generated from the raw trials |
+| `metadata.txt` | Campaign-level settings and plotting versions |
+| `MANIFEST.tsv` | Byte size and SHA-256 digest of every retained artifact |
+
+The CSV header is the authority for the exact schema. Every raw row records the implementation,
+family, format, stored width, operation, execution class, backend, measurement method, iterations,
+elapsed nanoseconds, timed sink and fixture-trace digest, and the separate fixed-prefix agreement
+count, sink, and fixture-trace digest.
+
+## Separate correctness campaigns
+
+The binary16 differential checker is a correctness campaign, not a timing baseline:
 
 ```bash
 tests/oracles/binary16-exhaustive.sh add SHARD_INDEX SHARD_COUNT
 tests/oracles/binary16-exhaustive.sh mul SHARD_INDEX SHARD_COUNT
 ```
+
+Its retained release rows are described in [`tests/EXTERNAL.md`](../../tests/EXTERNAL.md).
+
+## Reading the result honestly
+
+The public plot should be read with these limits in mind:
+
+- native C and SoftFloat use the same dependent-chain protocol as FloatLean; the result is not a
+  raw reciprocal-throughput chart;
+- the small-format rows include dispatch, table lookup, fixture selection, and sink overhead;
+- one FloatLean curve crosses explicitly labelled kernel boundaries and is not one algorithm at
+  every width;
+- MPFR and the Flocq precision model compare binary arithmetic at matched significand precision,
+  not every destination format's exceptional-value and exponent policy;
+- CPython measures a language runtime, while native C and SoftFloat are compiled adapters;
+- equal-storage posit comparisons are a convention because posit precision varies with value;
+- Stillwater rows validate only the retained benchmark workload, and rejected operations remain
+  visible as external limitations; and
+- low median absolute deviation does not remove systematic machine, scheduler, compiler, or
+  harness effects.
+
+The benchmark result can tell us where execution time goes and which backend needs work. The
+conformance campaign can catch disagreements with independent implementations. Neither one proves
+the generic theorems: those remain Lean kernel-checked statements about every value satisfying
+their hypotheses.
+
+When a benchmark report uses ecosystem labels such as `qualified-pass`,
+`observational-complete`, `unsupported`, `external-limitation`, `diagnostic-failure`, or
+`upstream-failure`, their exact definitions are in
+[`tests/EXTERNAL.md`](../../tests/EXTERNAL.md#status-labels). A timing row is never promoted to
+`pass` merely because it is fast.
