 ## The algebra that fails
 
@@ -321,6 +321,6 @@ The trust surface is small and named. What remains outside Lean's kernel is list
 
 We want to be exact about the boundary, because a machine-checked proof is worth only as much as the honesty of its trusted base. Below Lean's kernel and the three axioms above sit the things no theorem in this library covers: the Lean compiler and the proved `@[csimp]` equations it is trusted to honour, one `@[implemented_by]` substitution in the posit decoder, the `native_decide` sites confined to the conformance tests, and the host FPU behind `NativeFPU.Unchecked`. [Chapter 05](#/chapter/why-execution-and-proofs-are-separate) walks that list item by item, with the scripts that keep it from growing, and we do not repeat it here. The item that belongs to this chapter is the one a proof assistant cannot remove.
 
-We cannot prove the specification itself. A theorem shows that the implementation equals our reference and that our reference equals a rounding of the exact real result. It cannot show that our reading of IEEE 754 is the standard's. That is what the external campaigns of [chapter 11](#/chapter/validation-against-the-outside-world) are for. Berkeley TestFloat at level 1 ran 175 campaigns over binary16, binary32, binary64, binary128, and BF16 conversions, 102,454,320 cases in all, with zero value mismatches and zero flag mismatches, as recorded in `tests/EXTERNAL.md` for revision `009e4a9` on 2026-09-03; the ledger marks that row historical until it is rerun against the current tree, and [chapter 11](#/chapter/validation-against-the-outside-world) gives the details. Agreement with an independent implementation is evidence about the specification; the theorems are about the implementation.
+We cannot prove the specification itself. A theorem shows that the implementation equals our reference and that our reference equals a rounding of the exact real result. It cannot show that our reading of IEEE 754 is the standard's. That is what the external campaigns of [chapter 11](#/chapter/validation-against-the-outside-world) are for. The retained 2026-09-10 release campaign under `tests/results/main` keeps the TestFloat, MPFR, format-table, and host-comparison logs together with the source and environment metadata needed to interpret them. We take the status and scope of each run from that result bundle rather than copying unfinished or historical totals into this chapter. Agreement with an independent implementation supports our reading of the specification; it does not replace the refinement theorems, and a host result matching a proved software result is still a validation check rather than a proof about the host instruction.
 
 The approach is Flocq's [@boldoMelquiond2011], carried through to code that runs. [Chapter 03](#/chapter/what-imprecision-has-cost) asks what the effort buys, and [chapter 05](#/chapter/why-execution-and-proofs-are-separate) explains how we keep executable and proved code in one place without extraction.
