-How fast is a proved floating-point operation, and what does that number actually tell you? The second half of the question matters more than the first. The short answer to the first half, on our test machine: a proved binary16 addition takes about 18 ns, binary32 about 35 ns, binary64 about 280 ns, and binary128 about 3 microseconds, while the hardware adds two binary64 values in 1.25 ns and MPFR does it in software in 16.5 ns (the software figures are from our runs of 2026-09-07 with the pass applied; the hardware and MPFR figures are from the retained bundle, measured earlier, pinned, on a quieter machine, so the two sets are not one measurement, and [What we measured and how](#/chapter/performance/what-we-measured-and-how) gives the conditions of each). The rest of this chapter is about what those numbers mean and what they leave out. A refinement theorem such as [[LeanFloat.Floats.ExecFloat.Proof.add_eq_spec]] fixes the result of `x + y` for every input, and it does so for every kernel the planner might choose, because each candidate carries its own proof before the planner looks at it ([[LeanFloat.Floats.ExecFloat.Backend.selectCertified_run_eq_spec]]). A timing, on the other hand, depends on which kernel was selected, on the C code Lean generated for it, on the compiler, on the machine, and on what else the machine was doing. So every number in this chapter comes with the file it was read from and the conditions it was read under, and none of them is a claim about a later revision.
