-## What we measured and how
+`benchmarks/results/main/release/benchmark`
 
-All the timings here come from one harness, `benchmarks/scripts/format-comparison.sh`, which compares implementations at equal storage width: a 32 bit row means 32 encoded bits whether the row is our binary32, a posit32, MPFR at 24 bits of significand, or the host FPU. The inputs are the exact rationals in `benchmarks/lean/LeanFloatBenchmarks/Support/ExactWorkload.lean`, rounded once into the format during setup, outside the timed loop, so every implementation rounds the same exact values and no host `double` sneaks in as an intermediate. MPFR receives them through `mpfr_set_q` for the same reason (`benchmarks/docs/Comparison.md`).
+The campaign covers encoded widths from 2 through 4096 bits. That upper point is where we stopped
+this run, not a precision limit in FloatLean.
 
-Each implementation, width, and operation runs in a fresh process with its own iteration count. The count is calibrated from a pilot run so that the timed loop lasts about 200 ms:
+## The measurement we chose
+
+Every lane uses a result-dependent fixture chain. An operation consumes values from a small,
+ordinary fixture set; its result selects the next fixture; and the loop records both a final sink
+and a digest of the fixtures it visited. We wanted three things from that design:
+
+1. the compiler must not be able to replace the loop with a precomputed answer;
+2. native, software, language-runtime, and reference adapters must perform the same surrounding
+   work; and
+3. each trial must leave enough evidence to catch an adapter that silently followed another
+   workload.
+
+This is a latency-oriented scalar chain, following the dependent-chain distinction used by
+uops.info [@abelUopsInfo2019]. The reported time includes fixture selection, the arithmetic call,
+and checksum work. It is not raw instruction latency, reciprocal throughput, or the speed of a
+large vectorized loop. In particular, the harness is a visible part of a very small FP8 or posit
+operation.
+
+The inputs begin as exact rationals in
+`benchmarks/lean/LeanFloatBenchmarks/Support/ExactWorkload.lean`. Each adapter rounds them into its
+destination format before timing. MPFR receives the same rationals directly rather than receiving
+values that have already passed through a host `double`.
+
+Each format, operation, and implementation gets its own calibration. If a pilot performs
+$n_p$ operations in $t_p$ nanoseconds, the runner chooses
 
 $$
-n = \left\lceil \frac{2 \times 10^{8}\; n_{\text{pilot}}}{t_{\text{pilot}}} \right\rceil, \qquad 1 \le n \le 5 \times 10^{7},
+n = \left\lceil \frac{T n_p}{t_p} \right\rceil
 $$
 
-with $t_{\text{pilot}}$ in nanoseconds. We calibrate per row because a single count cannot serve both a 1.3 ns hardware addition and a 10 microsecond software division at 4096 bits. Every row is preceded by 256 warm-up iterations, except the extracted Flocq rows, whose warm-up is capped at 64, 16, 4, or 1 iterations as the width grows because one Flocq operation at the widest widths takes milliseconds (`benchmarks/scripts/format-comparison.sh`). Even numbered trials traverse the rows in reverse order to spread thermal and position effects. The statistic we quote is the median of nanoseconds per operation across trials, computed by `benchmarks/plots/format_comparison.py`, which also records the minimum, quartiles, and median absolute deviation in `summary.csv`. When a row's median absolute deviation is a large fraction of its median we say so, because that row is telling us about the machine rather than the code.
+for the campaign target $T$, subject to a documented safety cap. A public row must still exceed
+the publication duration floor. The retained campaign ran nine trials for every accepted cell and
+reports the median together with the 5th and 95th percentiles, quartiles, standard deviation, and
+median absolute deviation.
+
+One timed CPU was pinned inside a reserved 190-CPU evaluator pod. The other CPUs were used for
+building and for the independent conformance job. Pinning prevents migration of the timed process;
+it does not mean that the process owned an exclusive physical core or was isolated from shared
+cache, memory, frequency, and thermal effects. The retained provenance says exactly what the
+scheduler reserved and which CPU the process used.
