-Division is the slow operation at every width. Binary16 division takes 330 ns where binary16 addition takes 18.5 ns; it runs the parameterized native-word division kernel, not the monomorphic binary32 one that takes 63 ns, and we have not yet profiled why the parameterized kernel is five times slower on a smaller format. Binary64 division takes 1639 ns, a restoring quotient on `UInt64` that improved only 1.5 times. These are the next rows to work on.
+The obvious next performance work is therefore not another scalar wrapper. It is wider proved
+division and square-root kernels, less allocation in the general path, and first-order storage
+interfaces that keep fixed-width values unboxed.
 
-The 48 bit custom layout E11M36 exposes a hole in the ladder. Its addition selects the one-word kernel (35.3 ns) and its multiplication the two-word product, which the planner names the fixed-limb kernel (15.0 ns, the fastest multiplication in this chapter, faster than the monomorphic binary32 multiplication at 46.2 ns, and we have not yet worked out why), but its division drops to the exact baseline at 2163 ns (`pass5-final2-summary.csv`). The reason is in the contracts: the one-word division kernel [[LeanFloat.Floats.Formats.BinaryInterchange.Model.NativeSmallWord.Eligible]] accepts at most 30 fraction bits, the pair kernels require more than 64, and the fixed-format kernels in between are written for the binary32 and binary64 layouts only (`Descriptor/Plan/Routes.lean`), so a custom 36 bit fraction falls through to the exact baseline. Both eligibility facts are decidable propositions, so rather than read the kernel source we can have Lean check them. The two `example`s below are closed by `decide`: the first says the one-word add kernel accepts E11M36, the second says the one-word division kernel refuses it.
