-The TestFloat and MPFR comparisons, which carry nearly all of the case count, call the logical `Model` operations: status-bearing arithmetic goes through [[LeanFloat.Floats.Formats.BinaryInterchange.Model.addWithStatus]] and its siblings, comparisons go through [[LeanFloat.Floats.Formats.BinaryInterchange.Model.compare]], and the binary16 rows call [[LeanFloat.Floats.Formats.BinaryInterchange.Model.add]] and `mul` directly. They do not invoke the configured backends, deliberately. The backends are tied to the model by kernel-checked refinement theorems, so a backend disagreeing with the model would already be a proof failure and the build would not have finished. What the oracles can find is a place where the model itself, which the theorems take as ground truth, does not say what the standard says. An oracle disagreement is therefore a specification bug, not a proof bug. We fix it by correcting the general definition and adding a small format-independent regression, never by adding a named-format exception to satisfy the oracle.
