-## Berkeley TestFloat
-
-TestFloat [@hauserTestFloat] is the companion test generator to Berkeley SoftFloat [@hauserSoftFloat], John Hauser's software implementation of IEEE 754 arithmetic, which emulators and hardware projects use as a reference. Its `testfloat_gen` program emits, for one function and one rounding mode, a stream of operands with the result and the five IEEE exception flags that SoftFloat produced, and it deliberately concentrates on ties, cancellations, the edges of the subnormal range, and every combination of value classes. We chose it as our primary conformance oracle because it is independent, widely used to validate hardware, and checks flags, where informal implementations most often disagree with the standard.
-
-Our adapter is `tests/oracles/testfloat.sh` with the Lean checker in `tests/LeanFloatTests/Oracle/TestFloat.lean`. The script clones SoftFloat at commit `a0c6494` and TestFloat at commit `a9c849f`, refuses to run if either checkout has drifted, and pipes each generated stream into one Lean executable that handles binary16, binary32, binary64, and binary128 generically. Non-NaN results are compared bit for bit. NaNs are compared by class, quiet against signaling, because IEEE 754 does not prescribe which of a NaN's spare bits, its payload, survive an operation; whether the result is signaling, and whether invalid is raised, are still checked. All five flags (invalid, divide by zero, overflow, underflow, inexact) are compared, and we pass `-tininessafter` so that SoftFloat decides whether a result is tiny, below the smallest normal magnitude, after rounding rather than before, the convention our status code implements.
-
-TestFloat generates cases at two levels, chosen with its `-level` option. Level 1, the default, emits a bounded stream for each function and rounding mode, pattern tests mixed with weighted random operands; level 2 emits many more cases with better coverage, few for a binary32 square root and trillions for binary128 fused multiply-add. The full level-1 profile is a matrix of 175 campaigns that the script generates row by row into `campaigns.tsv`. For each of the four formats there are add, subtract, multiply, divide, and square root in four rounding directions, remainder in nearest-even, round-to-integral in four directions, and six comparison predicates: 31 campaigns per format, 124 in all. Thirty-five conversion campaigns cover the cross-format casts and the BF16 conversions. Sixteen fused multiply-add campaigns complete the count, and the `core` profile is the same matrix without them. The four directions are exactly the constructors of [[LeanFloat.Floats.Formats.BinaryInterchange.Model.IEEERoundingMode]]:
-
-```lean
-open LeanFloat.Floats.Formats.BinaryInterchange
-
-#eval Model.IEEERoundingMode.all
--- [LeanFloat.Floats.Formats.BinaryInterchange.Model.IEEERoundingMode.nearestEven,
---  LeanFloat.Floats.Formats.BinaryInterchange.Model.IEEERoundingMode.towardZero,
---  LeanFloat.Floats.Formats.BinaryInterchange.Model.IEEERoundingMode.towardPositiveInfinity,
---  LeanFloat.Floats.Formats.BinaryInterchange.Model.IEEERoundingMode.towardNegativeInfinity]
-```
-
-The complete level-1 run recorded in `tests/EXTERNAL.md` was made on 2026-09-03 at Lean revision `009e4a9` with a recorded worktree diff and seed 1, using the documented command's eight workers pinned to CPUs 0 to 15. It covered 102,454,320 cases with 0 value mismatches, 0 flag mismatches, and 0 parse errors. A selected level-2 run on the same day ran the complete level-2 generator for three functions: binary16 addition in nearest-even (1,254,528 cases), binary32 square root toward positive infinity (8,800 cases), and binary32 to BF16 in nearest-even (8,800 cases), for 1,272,128 cases and 0 mismatches. That row is "selected complete campaigns", not a level-2 matrix: level-2 binary128 FMA alone has 1,851,438,185,872 vectors for one rounding mode, so the script refuses a `core` or `full` level-2 run unless `--allow-huge-level2` is passed deliberately.
-
-The exclusions matter as much as the counts. We do not run binary80, the 80-bit x87 extended format, because we have no adapter for its explicit integer bit. We do not run BF16 arithmetic (bfloat16, the truncated binary32 used in machine learning), because the pinned TestFloat interface exposes only BF16 conversion campaigns. We do not run floating-point to integer conversions until the library has a matching public invalid-result policy. Nearest-away, round-to-odd, and tininess-before-rounding are not exposed as substitutes, and strict NaN payload selection is not claimed. Each exclusion is listed in `tests/EXTERNAL.md` so that nobody reads "175 campaigns passed" as "everything passed".
