-## Extracted Flocq: run beside us, not checked against
-
-Flocq [@boldoMelquiond2011] is the Coq floating-point library our rounding theory follows, and `benchmarks/scripts/rocq.sh` extracts Flocq 4.2.2's proved `Bplus`, `Bminus`, `Bmult`, `Bdiv`, `Bsqrt`, and `Bfma` to OCaml with Zarith integers in a pinned Coq 8.20.1 container. In the checked-in comparison it runs at every binary width from 4 through 4,096 at the matching significand precision, with Flocq's benchmark exponent bound `emax = 16384`, and `benchmarks/results/main/summary.csv` holds 84 Flocq rows, one per width and operation. The harness times this lane; it does not compare its outputs with ours. `benchmarks/docs/Comparison.md` presents Flocq as a binary arithmetic-cost reference, and there is no Flocq conformance row in `tests/EXTERNAL.md`. It is an independent proved implementation of the same mathematics run side by side, not an oracle we have checked against.
-
-## The host FPU boundary
-
-One more comparison sits between what we proved and what the machine executes. The suite `lake -d tests exe check native`, defined in `tests/LeanFloatTests/Conformance/BinaryInterchange/NativeExecution.lean`, checks 160 public dispatch results (six operations per width, binary32 and binary64, on fixtures spanning every value class) against the selected software kernels, compares 132 samples of the ten opt-in `NativeFPU.Unchecked` host operations with the proved kernels bit for bit, and round-trips 24 named encodings through Lean's native `Float32` and `Float`; the historical row in `tests/EXTERNAL.md` records all of them passing. A mismatch in the host half would mean the compiler, runtime, processor, or floating-point environment disagrees with the software kernel; the subnormal fixtures exist to catch flush-to-zero environments. Agreement validates the toolchain and hardware; it is not a refinement theorem, and no theorem in the library claims one. [Chapter 17](#/chapter/comparing-with-lean-native-floats) describes the fixtures, the guards around each host call, and the one place, NaN payloads, where host and library differ by design.
-
-## Our own suites: conformance proofs and regressions
-
-Two kinds of tests under `tests/` are not external at all. `LeanFloatTests/Conformance/` is checked at build time. Its files are mostly theorems about specific configured types, proved by kernel-checked means: `decide +kernel`, `rfl`, `grind`, term-mode proofs, and the library's own automation (`numerics`, `binary_interchange_spec`), with `native_decide` at the 79 approved sites. The directory also holds the executable native-boundary suite and the axiom audit, which are validation, not theorems. `Execution/` proves the expected backend selections and certificates. `BinaryInterchange/` and `Formats/` check boundary cases, conversions, and low-bit packaging, and `Numerics/`, `Posit/`, and `P3109/` cover their families. The one trust-surface item that lives in this directory is `native_decide`: we allow it only in conformance files, never in library semantics or backend proofs, and `tests/checks/trust-surface.sh` keeps an exact per-file count of those sites. [Chapter 05](#/chapter/why-execution-and-proofs-are-separate) describes that script, the axiom audit in `Trust/Axioms.lean`, and the rest of the trust surface.
-
