-## What passing means, and what it does not
-
-A clean external run means that on every generated case in the stated overlap, our model produced the same bits, the same NaN class, and the same five flags as an implementation we did not write. For TestFloat's level-1 matrix that overlap is 102,454,320 cases across four formats from a generator that is adversarial about boundaries, so a specification error in any covered operation, rounding direction, or flag would have had to hide in the uncovered corners. Passing does not mean those corners are empty. It does not cover the TestFloat exclusions listed above, and nothing covers decimal formats, P3109 arithmetic, OCP MX block operations, or correctly rounded transcendentals; `tests/EXTERNAL.md` lists every gap with its reason, which ranges from a missing implementation or adapter to a comparison we have not yet made or a public policy we have not yet fixed. The picture below puts the case count of every row of the ledger on one logarithmic axis, split by format or operation where the ledger or the corpus files give the split.
-
-![Checked cases per oracle on a logarithmic axis, split by format or operation where the ledger records the split, from tests/EXTERNAL.md and the conformance files it names](assets/ch11-oracle-coverage.png)
-
-Passing also says nothing about the proofs, in either direction. The theorems would be exactly as true if every oracle disagreed with us; they would just be theorems about a specification nobody else uses. The oracles would be exactly as satisfied if a theorem were missing; they only see results. The two kinds of evidence answer different questions, and we need both.
-
-Finally, the ledger is honest about time. Every row in `tests/EXTERNAL.md` is historical unless its basis names HEAD. The complete TestFloat and P3109 runs record revision `009e4a9` with a worktree diff hash; the MPFR, Arb, and host-boundary rows did not retain their exact basis, and the `/tmp` artifacts from all of them are gone. Our rule is that such rows must be rerun before they are described as validation of HEAD, and this chapter follows it: the numbers above are what those runs produced on the revisions they name. Green CI is the smoke check named above, not a rerun of those campaigns. The checked-in performance bundle carries the same caveat; its source metadata records commit `3c91662` with 504 to 515 dirty files.
