+This protocol measures an end-to-end scalar chain. Each reported operation includes fixture selection, the lane's representation and adapter work, the arithmetic call, and result observation. It does not measure the latency of one bare hardware instruction, and it does not measure reciprocal throughput from a loop of independent operations that a processor can overlap. The native C lane is useful because it runs through the same harness as the software lanes. It should not be read as a processor data-sheet number.
 
-Two further caveats. The unchecked API itself has no row in any checked-in bundle: `benchmarks/lean/LeanFloatBenchmarks/Kernels/ConfiguredBinary.lean` keeps it outside the abstraction-cost benchmark on purpose, and the C lane is the hardware ceiling, not a timing of the unchecked functions themselves, whose compiled calls add the guard, a bit cast in each direction, and whatever boxing the surrounding code imposes. And a ratio against the FPU is the wrong number for deciding whether our binary64 is fast enough for a task; the right one is the absolute cost, 276 ns per addition in the later run, set against what the rest of the program spends per element.
