-Every Lean file in the library begins with `module`. Files with declarations open `@[expose] public section`, or `public meta section` for elaborator code, before the first one, and each import is `public import` when it is part of the file's interface and a plain `import` when it is only needed to build the proofs. This is Lean's module system, and the architecture check requires the `module` line and a `/-!` docstring in every source file. We adopted it for the reason the numbers in `CONTRIBUTING.md` make plain: profiling `Rounding/Policy/Agreement.lean` gave 15.4 seconds of import against 24.4 seconds of wall time, and the import closure of the library was 5003 modules, 2321 of them Mathlib, as `CONTRIBUTING.md` counted it at `fb21af8`; the pass's 20 new modules brought that to the 5023 that [chapter 05](#/chapter/why-execution-and-proofs-are-separate) reports. Those import-closure figures have not been remeasured on `6e98477`, which has 796 library modules. The layers a program can stop at sit between those ends, counted with `env.header.moduleNames.size` after each import: the execution-only aggregate `ExecFloat/Runtime.lean` loads 2262 modules, 520 of them from Mathlib, and `Formats/BinaryInterchange/Configured/Type.lean`, the module that defines the configured binary formats, loads 3812 with 1744 from Mathlib, among them `Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic` and `Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Pow.Real`, because its own runtime modules import them for the descriptor and `toReal` statements. Loading imports dominates everything else, and the module system is what lets a file say which of its imports are part of its interface, so a client loads only those.
+Every Lean file in the library begins with `module`. Files with declarations open `@[expose] public section`, or `public meta section` for elaborator code, before the first one, and each import is `public import` when it is part of the file's interface and a plain `import` when it is needed only to build the implementation or proof. The architecture check requires the `module` line and a `/-!` docstring in every source file. We adopted this discipline after profiling showed that loading imports, especially proof dependencies, dominated several representative builds. The measurements in `CONTRIBUTING.md` remain useful as dated engineering notes, but we do not present them as permanent properties of the current tree. The design point is stable: public imports describe the interface, private imports stay local, and clients can stop at the lightest layer that provides the definitions they need.
 
-Aggregator modules worked against that. An aggregator is a file that contains nothing but imports, `Formats/Posit/Quire.lean`, which forwarded the public quire facets, for instance. Its convenience has three costs. It pulls every facet into every client, so a runtime module that needed one decoder also loaded the proof modules and the Mathlib analysis behind them. It hides that fact from the reader and from the architecture check. And it defeats dependency minimization, because `lake shake`, the tool that removes imports a file does not use, cannot remove one that a forwarding file makes indirectly necessary. Two earlier passes are recorded in the message of commit `e4c9a2f`: replacing the umbrella `import Mathlib.Tactic` in 27 proof files with the tactic modules they use dropped about 580 Mathlib modules from every downstream load, and a conservative shake pass with `-- shake:` annotations on the intentional aggregators cut the visible modules per file by a quarter. The last step is the commit `fb21af8`, which deletes 89 Lean files under `LeanFloat/` (`git show fb21af8 --diff-filter=D --name-only` lists them). Every one was a forwarding file: a copyright header, a `module` line (fourteen still annotated `-- shake: keep-all`), imports, a module docstring, and in 26 cases an `@[expose] public section` opener, with nothing declared. Five of them forwarded inspection modules with `public meta import`; the other 84 were plain `public import` lists. The forwarding files that pulled `Proof` facets into runtime clients are gone, and `CONTRIBUTING.md` asks contributors to remove obsolete forwarding files rather than add new ones.
+Import-only aggregators work against that design when they forward unrelated facets. A runtime module that needs one decoder can otherwise inherit proof modules and the Mathlib analysis behind them without saying so. The extra layer also hides the real dependency from readers and gives `lake shake` less room to remove unused imports. We therefore keep umbrellas only where they are deliberate public entry points: the repository root, layer roots, format-family roots, examples, and a small number of sub-family entry points. An intentional umbrella carries `-- shake: keep-all`; obsolete forwarding files are removed instead of kept as aliases.
 
-What remains is the root `LeanFloat.lean` and 47 import-only modules under `LeanFloat/`: the layer roots `Numerics.lean`, `Kernels.lean`, `Floats.lean`, `Floats/Formats.lean`, `Floats/ExecFloat.lean`, and `Floats/Interval.lean`, one module per format family, `Examples.lean`, and sub-family entry points such as `Numerics/Core.lean`, `Kernels/FixedWord.lean`, and `BinaryInterchange/Configured.lean`. Eighteen of them carry `module -- shake: keep-all`, the annotation that tells the shake pass an import list is the interface and not an accident. The architecture check requires the root `LeanFloat.lean`, the layer roots, and `Examples.lean` to exist, and derives the expected import list of `Formats.lean` from the directories actually present, so a family cannot be added without being registered. It also requires every module in the tree to be reachable from a maintained root, so a stale experiment cannot hide unreferenced, and it requires internal modules to import the `ExecFloat.Core` and `Posit.Model` facets they use rather than the whole namespace.
