@@ -27,7 +27,7 @@ We work with numerical code, and the formats that code runs in have multiplied.
 
 The trouble is that floating point resists testing. Binary64 addition alone has $2^{128}$ input pairs, so no test suite finishes, and the bugs that matter, a double rounding here, a sticky bit dropped there, live in the corners that random testing rarely visits. A proof assistant is the one tool that can close that gap, but the usual way to use one is to prove a model of the arithmetic and then extract or reimplement the code that actually runs, so the code is a second artifact that has to be trusted to match the model. We wanted the same definitions to be the thing that executes and the thing the theorems are about, with Lean's kernel checking the equation between them.
 
-So the library is built around one convention, explained in [chapter 05](#/chapter/why-execution-and-proofs-are-separate): executable kernels live in one file, theorems about them in a sibling, and every kernel we ship carries a kernel checked equation saying it computes exactly the specification. The format is a parameter. Binary16 through binary128, the machine learning formats, posits, the P3109 draft, fixed point, logarithmic, and block scaled numbers all go through the same rounding theory, proved once. We then did the two things a proof cannot do for you. We ran the specification against the outside world at an earlier revision (`009e4a9`): 102,454,320 Berkeley TestFloat cases with zero disagreements among them, plus MPFR and Arb ([chapter 11](#/chapter/validation-against-the-outside-world)). Those campaigns have not been rerun on the current tree. And we measured: a proved binary32 addition takes about 35 nanoseconds on our test machine, and [chapter 10](#/chapter/performance) puts that number beside MPFR, extracted Flocq, Stillwater Universal and the hardware, with every caveat the measurement carries.
+So the library is built around one convention, explained in [chapter 05](#/chapter/why-execution-and-proofs-are-separate): executable kernels live in one file, theorems about them in a sibling, and every kernel we ship carries a kernel checked equation saying it computes exactly the specification. The format is a parameter. Binary16 through binary128, the machine learning formats, posits, the P3109 draft, fixed point, logarithmic, and block scaled numbers all go through the same rounding theory, proved once. We then did the two things a proof cannot do for us. We ran a retained release campaign against TestFloat, SoftFloat, MPFR, ONNX, P3109 tables, Arb, IBM FPgen, Z3, SoftPosit, and direct transcendental references, and we kept the broader FPBench, CORE-MATH, Daisy, FPTaylor, Herbie, RLIBM, OpenLibm, FLiT, Verificarlo, ReproBLAS, and ExBLAS results even where an upstream limitation makes the outcome qualified ([chapter 11](#/chapter/validation-against-the-outside-world)). We also reran the scalar comparison from 2 through 4,096 bits against MPFR, Berkeley SoftFloat, extracted Flocq, Stillwater Universal, native C, and CPython. [Chapter 10](#/chapter/performance) reads those retained rows directly and carries the workload, affinity, and backend caveats with every conclusion.
 
 Every Lean example in these pages is compiled against the library when the site is built. A `-- ` comment after an `#eval` is what the code printed, not what we hoped it would print.
 
