+## What kind of evidence is this?
+
+We keep the evidence classes separate because they answer different questions.
+
+| Evidence class | What it checks | What it does not establish |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Differential conformance | FloatLean and an independent implementation return the same encoded value, class, or flags over a stated overlap | Correctness outside the generated cases or unsupported policies |
+| Published-table conformance | FloatLean decodes every retained code from an independently published table | Arithmetic behavior not represented by that table |
+| Numerical reference validation | FloatLean agrees with exact, high-precision, or enclosing reference values over a stated corpus | A global error bound or correctly rounded implementation |
+| Observational comparison | We ran the planned corpus and retained every numerical difference | Pass/fail conformance when the external libraries implement different contracts |
+| Boundary validation | A native or foreign implementation agrees with the proved software model on sampled calls | A refinement theorem for the compiler, ABI, host FPU, or foreign library |
+| Upstream qualification | An external project builds and passes its own tests, examples, or benchmark gates in our pinned environment | Direct agreement with FloatLean unless a separate adapter performs that comparison |
+| Performance measurement | Implementations execute the same retained workload under one measurement protocol | Semantic correctness, proof coverage, or raw hardware instruction latency |
+
+The class belongs to the row, not merely to the tool. For example, MPFR is a numerical reference
+for finite arithmetic, while Berkeley TestFloat is the independent IEEE oracle for result bits and
+exception flags.
