+## Wider ecosystem campaign
 
-## Coverage not currently claimed
+The ecosystem tree answers a broader question: can we build and exercise the tools people use for
+floating-point testing, rewriting, error analysis, reproducibility, and numerical-library work in
+the same pinned release environment?
 
-| Area | Reason |
+| Family | What we retain |
 | --- | --- |
-| IEEE decimal32/64/128 | No decimal-format implementation |
-| Binary80 | No explicit-integer-bit encoding adapter |
-| Integer conversions | No matching public invalid-result and range policy |
-| Additional rounding policies | Nearest-away, round-to-odd, and tininess-before are not exposed as substitutes |
-| NaN payload identity | The current policy checks NaN class and signaling, not one universal payload-selection rule |
-| Complete P3109 arithmetic | Current external coverage is exact draft-table decoding |
-| Complete OCP MX interoperability | Block operations have not yet been compared with an external implementation |
-| Official posit certification | No official comprehensive certification suite is available |
-| Correctly rounded transcendentals | Current implementations do not yet have global correct-rounding or real-error proofs |
-| Large verified BLAS | Scalar and exact-reduction foundations exist; optimized GEMV/GEMM refinement remains future work |
-
-## Updating this ledger
-
-For every new run:
-
-1. choose a new output directory rather than overwriting an earlier result;
-2. record the clean commit or describe the dirty worktree exactly;
-3. pin all external revisions;
-4. keep worker and CPU limits explicit;
-5. retain the generated machine-readable summary;
-6. add or update one row in **Results already obtained**; and
-7. update the maintained-suite table only when the runnable scope changes.
-
-If a run exposes a bug, first add a small format-independent regression, then fix the general
-specification or implementation. Do not add a named-format exception merely to satisfy an oracle.
+| FPBench | Upstream workflow and standalone diagnostics; no direct FloatLean comparison |
+| Daisy and FPTaylor | Upstream analysis and regression suites; no direct FloatLean comparison |
+| Herbie | Upstream tests and workload observations; no direct FloatLean comparison |
+| OpenLibm and RLIBM | Upstream gates plus a separate direct transcendental observation where adapters overlap |
+| CORE-MATH | All 169 available binary16, bfloat16, binary32, and binary64 function gates; 166 passed and three retained build limitations require a newer `mpfr_compound` |
+| Verificarlo | Upstream instrumentation diagnostics; no direct FloatLean comparison |
+| FLiT | The complete 140-configuration GCC/Clang litmus matrix in base, OpenMPI, and MPICH modes, with every final comparison CSV and imported SQLite database retained |
+| ReproBLAS and ExBLAS | Upstream reproducible or exact-reduction gates and secondary diagnostics |
+| SMT QF_FP | Direct FloatLean/Z3 exact-bit and NaN-class differential results |
+
+There is no single pass condition shared by these projects. The retained
+[`ecosystem/summary.tsv`](results/main/ecosystem/summary.tsv) assigns one of the status labels
+above, and each row points to its log. Suite directories keep the pinned revision, build command,
+patch or compatibility note when one was needed, and the outputs used to classify the row.
+
+The CORE-MATH row is qualified rather than silently green: three compound-interest functions
+cannot link against the campaign image's MPFR 4.2.1 because that release does not export
+`mpfr_compound`. The other 166 functions passed their upstream gates. FLiT's three rows are plain
+passes. We disabled FLiT's timing loop because this campaign asks whether compiler configurations
+change outputs, not which compiler wins a timing contest; all 140 final comparison CSVs per mode
+and the queryable databases remain in the package. FLiT's generated Makefile deletes the earlier
+`*-out` intermediates after their comparison CSV targets are complete.
+
+Qualified and failed diagnostics remain in the package. They show whether FloatLean disagreed with
+an oracle, an optional diagnostic was stricter than the primary gate, or the upstream project did
+not support the requested combination.
+
+## Coverage we do not claim
+
+The release campaign does not claim:
+
+- IEEE decimal32, decimal64, or decimal128;
+- binary80's explicit-integer-bit representation;
+- every floating-point/integer conversion policy;
+- nearest-away, round-to-odd, or tininess-before-rounding;
+- one universal NaN payload-selection rule;
+- complete P3109 arithmetic or OCP MX interoperability;
+- official posit certification;
+- global correct-rounding or real-error theorems for all transcendentals; or
+- an optimized verified BLAS implementation.
+
+The exact reduction and matrix foundations in the library are useful starting points, but they do
+not turn scalar differential tests into a verified GEMM theorem.
+
+## Why these tests do not replace proofs
+
+The Lean kernel checks theorems about the definitions in this repository. External campaigns check
+things those theorems cannot see directly: imported tables, parsers, generated streams, C and
+Python adapters, compiler output, host arithmetic, foreign libraries, and pinned upstream tools.
+
+The converse matters too. Even an exhaustive test over one finite format says nothing about every
+width, descriptor, or policy in a generic theorem. A million matching samples are still samples.
+We use the external campaign to catch integration mistakes and challenge the executable boundary;
+we use proofs for the format-general statements.
+
+When an external run finds a bug, we first add a small format-independent regression. Then we fix
+the general specification or implementation. We do not add a named-format exception merely to
+make one oracle row green.
